1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,611
A security reviewer asks your team: "Your
commits are verified, so you know who

2
00:00:09,651 --> 00:00:14,305
wrote the code that runs in production,
correct?" Is the reviewer right?

3
00:00:14,345 --> 00:00:15,870
Say yes or no, out loud.

4
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:25,383
No. There are two things wrong with that
sentence, and a third that's wrong with the

5
00:00:25,423 --> 00:00:29,817
commit list. The badge is a statement
about a key and an account, not about who

6
00:00:29,857 --> 00:00:33,071
typed the change. The commits
with no badge aren't "fine".

7
00:00:33,111 --> 00:00:36,495
They're unsigned, and by default
GitHub is silent about them.

8
00:00:36,835 --> 00:00:41,827
And one of the most common ways to merge a
pull request, GitHub's proposal to merge

9
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:46,920
one branch into another, produces commits
that aren't signed by their author at all.

10
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:50,861
Which one? Keep that question.
You'll derive the answer yourself.

11
00:00:52,701 --> 00:00:56,357
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:56,397 --> 00:01:01,203
In the last two videos everything was Git:
a signature in an object, verified on

13
00:01:01,243 --> 00:01:06,110
your machine against a file you control.
A signature is made with a key, over the

14
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:10,411
bytes of one commit, and a commit is
one saved snapshot of the project.

15
00:01:10,451 --> 00:01:12,532
This video crosses to the platform.

16
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:18,385
The badge next to a commit is GitHub's
judgment about a signature and an account.

17
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:24,144
GitHub runs its own verification, against
keys registered on accounts, and shows the

18
00:01:24,184 --> 00:01:29,423
result. You'll learn the states it can
show, what vigilant mode changes, what

19
00:01:29,463 --> 00:01:35,113
persistent verification records are, which
commits GitHub signs itself, why "rebase

20
00:01:35,153 --> 00:01:40,460
and merge" yields unsigned commits, and
what the "require signed commits" rule

21
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,396
enforces. And then the limits, with
the case the textbook uses for them.

22
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:49,890
The local replay builds three
commits and shows what Git recorded.

23
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:54,084
What GitHub displays for them is
described from the documentation.

24
00:01:54,124 --> 00:01:56,371
You observe it yourself in Lab 24.3.

25
00:01:58,211 --> 00:01:59,789
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:02:00,129 --> 00:02:01,542
After this video you can:

27
00:02:01,882 --> 00:02:05,709
name the verification states GitHub
displays and what produces each;

28
00:02:06,049 --> 00:02:10,807
say what vigilant mode and persistent
verification records change, as the section

29
00:02:10,847 --> 00:02:11,517
states them;

30
00:02:11,857 --> 00:02:14,108
say which commits GitHub signs itself;

31
00:02:14,448 --> 00:02:18,786
explain why "rebase and merge" produces
commits that are not signed by the author;

32
00:02:19,126 --> 00:02:22,384
state three things a "Verified"
badge does not establish.

33
00:02:24,224 --> 00:02:28,849
In one sentence. GitHub checks each commit's
signature against keys registered on

34
00:02:28,889 --> 00:02:33,571
accounts and shows the result as a badge.
Without vigilant mode an unsigned commit

35
00:02:33,611 --> 00:02:34,781
gets no badge at all.

36
00:02:35,121 --> 00:02:39,103
Precisely. GitHub verifies GPG,
SSH and S/MIME signatures.

37
00:02:39,143 --> 00:02:44,305
A cryptographically verifiable signature
is marked "Verified" or "Partially

38
00:02:44,345 --> 00:02:48,605
verified". A signature that can't
be verified is "Unverified".

39
00:02:48,645 --> 00:02:51,795
An unsigned commit shows
no status by default.

40
00:02:52,135 --> 00:02:55,775
For SSH there's a detail that
catches people on the first day.

41
00:02:55,815 --> 00:02:59,395
The public key must be added to
the account as a signing key.

42
00:02:59,435 --> 00:03:04,261
That's a separate registration from an
authentication key, even when it's the same

43
00:03:04,301 --> 00:03:04,795
key file.

44
00:03:05,135 --> 00:03:08,815
The command is shown as the
textbook shows it, without output.

45
00:03:08,855 --> 00:03:12,895
It changes your account, so it's
yours to run, in your normal shell.

46
00:03:13,235 --> 00:03:18,320
Vigilant mode changes what silence means.
The setting is called "Flag unsigned

47
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:23,379
commits as unverified" and lives in your
account's SSH and GPG keys settings.

48
00:03:23,719 --> 00:03:28,306
Without vigilant mode: "Verified" means
signed, and the signature verifies.

49
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:33,117
"Partially verified" isn't shown.
"Unverified" means signed, but the signature

50
00:03:33,157 --> 00:03:36,078
couldn't be verified. No
badge means not signed.

51
00:03:36,418 --> 00:03:41,138
With vigilant mode enabled by the person
the commit names: "Verified" means signed

52
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:45,841
and verified, and the committer is the
only author who has enabled vigilant mode.

53
00:03:45,881 --> 00:03:50,485
"Partially verified" means signed and
verified, but the commit has an author who

54
00:03:50,525 --> 00:03:53,620
isn't the committer and who
has enabled vigilant mode.

55
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:57,974
"Unverified" now also covers any unsigned
commit attributed to that person.

56
00:03:58,014 --> 00:04:01,167
And "no badge" doesn't occur
for that person's commits.

57
00:04:01,507 --> 00:04:06,311
The textbook calls vigilant mode the cheap
defense against the forgery of the last

58
00:04:06,351 --> 00:04:10,978
video. Once Asha enables it, the commit
that claims her name without her key is

59
00:04:11,018 --> 00:04:15,823
displayed as "Unverified", instead of
looking like all her other unsigned commits.

60
00:04:15,863 --> 00:04:20,608
One condition: enable it only after you
sign on every machine you commit from, or

61
00:04:20,648 --> 00:04:22,085
your own work is flagged.

62
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:27,597
Persistent verification records. Since the
tenth of December 2024, once GitHub has

63
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:32,554
verified a signature, the commit stays
verified within its repository network.

64
00:04:32,594 --> 00:04:37,892
A record is stored with the commit. It can't
be edited, and it persists when the key

65
00:04:37,932 --> 00:04:42,468
is later rotated, revoked or expired,
or when the contributor leaves the

66
00:04:42,508 --> 00:04:47,425
organization. GitHub doesn't re-verify
old commits when a key's state changes.

67
00:04:47,465 --> 00:04:52,256
The time of verification is exposed as
verified_at in the REST API, GitHub's

68
00:04:52,296 --> 00:04:53,717
interface for programs.

69
00:04:54,057 --> 00:04:57,621
Two consequences, one good and
one to remember in an incident.

70
00:04:57,661 --> 00:05:01,167
Rotating a key no longer turns
years of history "Unverified".

71
00:05:01,207 --> 00:05:05,584
But revoking a stolen key doesn't
un-verify what an attacker signed with it.

72
00:05:05,624 --> 00:05:09,653
You find those commits by date and
by the audit log, not by the badge.

73
00:05:09,993 --> 00:05:15,585
Quick quiz. You merge a pull request with
"Squash and merge" in the web interface.

74
00:05:15,625 --> 00:05:19,294
Whose key signs the new
commit? A, yours. B, GitHub's.

75
00:05:19,334 --> 00:05:21,012
C, nobody's. Your answer?

76
00:05:25,852 --> 00:05:30,798
B, GitHub's. Commits you make in the web
interface are signed by GitHub with its own

77
00:05:30,838 --> 00:05:35,784
key. That includes the merge commit of
"Create a merge commit" and the single commit

78
00:05:35,824 --> 00:05:40,532
of "Squash and merge". The textbook is
exact about what that badge means: GitHub

79
00:05:40,572 --> 00:05:45,340
created the commit on behalf of a signed-in
user. It doesn't mean that the user's

80
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:50,089
key was involved. Dependabot signs its
commits by default, and the Copilot cloud

81
00:05:50,129 --> 00:05:53,591
agent has signed its commits
since the third of April 2026.

82
00:05:53,931 --> 00:05:58,556
Why "Rebase and merge" yields unsigned
commits. You can derive this from what you

83
00:05:58,596 --> 00:06:03,163
already know. A rebase creates new commits
with new parents and a new committer.

84
00:06:03,203 --> 00:06:07,655
A signature covers the commit's content
including its parents, so the original

85
00:06:07,695 --> 00:06:12,378
signatures can't be carried over. And GitHub
doesn't have your private key to make

86
00:06:12,418 --> 00:06:16,870
new ones. The documentation states the
result: the commits are created without

87
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:20,614
signature verification. That's the
merge method from the opening.

88
00:06:20,954 --> 00:06:24,411
The require-signed-commits rule.
Display isn't enforcement.

89
00:06:24,451 --> 00:06:29,095
A ruleset is a named list of rules, and
its rule "Require signed commits" means

90
00:06:29,135 --> 00:06:33,422
contributors and bots can push only
commits that are signed and verified.

91
00:06:33,462 --> 00:06:38,047
Under rulesets GitHub checks only the
commits that aren't reachable from other

92
00:06:38,087 --> 00:06:42,967
branches. Put that together with the previous
paragraph: the rule makes "Rebase and

93
00:06:43,007 --> 00:06:47,532
merge" unusable on the protected branch,
while squash and merge commits pass,

94
00:06:47,572 --> 00:06:49,073
because GitHub signs them.

95
00:06:49,413 --> 00:06:53,525
One caveat that the textbook
marks as unverified, and so do I.

96
00:06:53,565 --> 00:06:58,748
Sigstore's keyless gitsign signs with
short-lived certificates tied to an OIDC

97
00:06:58,788 --> 00:07:04,038
identity, and its README says GitHub
doesn't display those commits as verified.

98
00:07:04,078 --> 00:07:09,663
The Phase 0 report couldn't re-verify this,
or GitHub's support for SSH certificates

99
00:07:09,703 --> 00:07:12,811
in verification, against
a 2026 primary source.

100
00:07:14,651 --> 00:07:19,578
Keep the wax seal from the last video: the
seal proves which seal pressed the wax.

101
00:07:19,618 --> 00:07:24,121
GitHub adds a registry to that picture.
The registry says which account has

102
00:07:24,161 --> 00:07:29,028
registered which seal. The badge is the
registry's answer to one question: is the

103
00:07:29,068 --> 00:07:32,541
seal on this object one that
the named account registered?

104
00:07:32,881 --> 00:07:37,746
Where the model breaks: a registry suggests
that the answer is looked up fresh each

105
00:07:37,786 --> 00:07:42,414
time. It isn't. With persistent verification
records the answer is written down

106
00:07:42,454 --> 00:07:46,905
once, at the time of verification, and
kept. And for commits made in the web

107
00:07:46,945 --> 00:07:49,919
interface the seal is
GitHub's own, not the user's.

108
00:07:50,259 --> 00:07:55,109
Section 21B.7 states the limit in one
sentence. A verified signature proves one

109
00:07:55,149 --> 00:07:59,999
thing: the holder of a particular private
key created this exact commit object.

110
00:08:00,039 --> 00:08:03,960
It doesn't prove that the change
is correct, reviewed or benign.

111
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,302
It doesn't prove that the
key holder is who you think.

112
00:08:07,342 --> 00:08:12,130
It doesn't prove that the person had not
been turned or impersonated socially.

113
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:15,675
The case to remember is the
xz-utils backdoor of 2024.

114
00:08:15,715 --> 00:08:20,603
It was introduced by a contributor who had
earned commit rights over more than two

115
00:08:20,643 --> 00:08:25,651
years of useful work, and the build script
that activated it was present only in the

116
00:08:25,691 --> 00:08:30,278
release tarball, not in the Git repository.
Every commit could have carried a

117
00:08:30,318 --> 00:08:34,785
perfect signature. Signing would have
changed nothing, for two reasons that

118
00:08:34,825 --> 00:08:35,446
generalize.

119
00:08:35,786 --> 00:08:39,405
First, the signer was authorized.
Signatures authenticate.

120
00:08:39,445 --> 00:08:44,643
They don't judge. Review, and limits on
what one maintainer can ship alone, are the

121
00:08:44,683 --> 00:08:46,599
controls for a trusted insider.

122
00:08:46,939 --> 00:08:51,177
Second, the artifact wasn't the
repository. What users ran was a tarball.

123
00:08:51,217 --> 00:08:56,041
A signature on commits says nothing about
a release archive, a wheel or a container

124
00:08:56,081 --> 00:09:00,671
image built somewhere else. That gap is
closed by build provenance and artifact

125
00:09:00,711 --> 00:09:03,660
attestations, which belong
to the Actions chapters.

126
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,886
The textbook's summary: signing removes
cheap impersonation and anchors the audit

127
00:09:08,926 --> 00:09:10,224
trail. It's one layer.

128
00:09:10,564 --> 00:09:13,883
Try it now, thirty seconds,
on paper. Three commits.

129
00:09:13,923 --> 00:09:18,985
Your own, unsigned. Your own, signed. And
one that names a colleague as author,

130
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:24,282
unsigned, pushed by you. For each, write
what GitHub is documented to display once

131
00:09:24,322 --> 00:09:27,770
the named person has enabled
vigilant mode. I'll wait.

132
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:38,511
Check your paper against the bottom
row: Unverified, Verified, Unverified.

133
00:09:38,551 --> 00:09:41,752
The third only if the colleague
enabled vigilant mode.

134
00:09:41,792 --> 00:09:44,933
Now look at commit 1 and
commit 3 in the default row.

135
00:09:44,973 --> 00:09:49,855
They look the same: no badge. That's the
problem vigilant mode addresses, and it's

136
00:09:49,895 --> 00:09:53,576
switched on by the person who is
named, not by the repository.

137
00:09:55,416 --> 00:09:58,385
Step 1: three commits with
three identity claims.

138
00:09:58,725 --> 00:10:02,397
Into the lab. git commit adds
an object and moves the branch.

139
00:10:02,437 --> 00:10:07,509
Commit A is the lab user's. In B, the author
is a claim and the committer is the lab

140
00:10:07,549 --> 00:10:09,639
user. In C, both fields are claims.

141
00:10:09,979 --> 00:10:11,836
Step 2: what Git recorded.

142
00:10:12,176 --> 00:10:16,796
Predict the three lines: author address,
committer address, and signature letter for

143
00:10:16,836 --> 00:10:18,460
A, B and C. Say them out loud.

144
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:29,623
fd9016a, c00065f and 0b4e72d. Three
different combinations of addresses, and the

145
00:10:29,663 --> 00:10:34,873
same letter three times: N. Git
recorded exactly what it was told.

146
00:10:35,213 --> 00:10:37,610
Step 3: there is nothing to verify.

147
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:42,845
Four header lines, no gpgsig. git
verify-commit prints nothing and exits with 1.

148
00:10:42,885 --> 00:10:48,027
Now connect this to the platform. By email
matching, GitHub would attribute commit C

149
00:10:48,067 --> 00:10:53,147
to whichever account owns Asha's address.
With no signature, the documented default

150
00:10:53,187 --> 00:10:57,342
is no badge. So C would look like
any other unsigned commit of hers.

151
00:10:57,682 --> 00:11:02,637
Step 4: the lab's failure scenario.
Signing is switched on before a key is

152
00:11:02,677 --> 00:11:03,380
configured.

153
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:07,790
git config set is 🟡 CAUTION: it
changes a configuration file.

154
00:11:07,830 --> 00:11:13,315
The commit fails with the message you met
in video 137, and the two lines after it

155
00:11:13,355 --> 00:11:18,975
are the important ones: the last commit is
still 0b4e72d, and the change is still in

156
00:11:19,015 --> 00:11:24,433
the working tree. Nothing was lost. The
lab's recovery section shows the way out.

157
00:11:24,473 --> 00:11:25,444
Do it yourself.

158
00:11:25,784 --> 00:11:30,673
Part B of Lab 24.3, on your own practice
repository, in your normal shell.

159
00:11:30,713 --> 00:11:36,202
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

160
00:11:36,242 --> 00:11:41,464
No GitHub output was captured by the
authors. Never show a real key or token on

161
00:11:41,504 --> 00:11:41,930
screen.

162
00:11:42,270 --> 00:11:46,891
You need a signing key in your normal
Git configuration and an address that's

163
00:11:46,931 --> 00:11:51,189
verified on your account. You register
the public key as a signing key.

164
00:11:51,229 --> 00:11:56,273
Then the lab has you make three empty commits
on a branch: one unsigned, one signed,

165
00:11:56,313 --> 00:12:00,207
and one unsigned whose author is
a made-up person at example.com.

166
00:12:00,247 --> 00:12:02,084
You look at them locally first:

167
00:12:02,424 --> 00:12:06,924
git push is 🟡 CAUTION. Before you open
the browser, write down what you expect

168
00:12:06,964 --> 00:12:08,845
beside each of the three commits.

169
00:12:09,185 --> 00:12:13,600
Then open the commit list of the branch.
For each commit, find the place where a

170
00:12:13,640 --> 00:12:17,777
verification badge would be, and note
whether there's one and what it says.

171
00:12:17,817 --> 00:12:22,288
Open the badge of the signed commit and
read what GitHub tells you about the key.

172
00:12:22,328 --> 00:12:26,966
The lab also reads the same information
through the REST API, as two fields: whether

173
00:12:27,006 --> 00:12:29,027
the commit is verified, and a reason.

174
00:12:29,367 --> 00:12:33,552
Then switch on vigilant mode in your
account settings, as the documentation

175
00:12:33,592 --> 00:12:37,045
describes, reload the commit
list, and note every badge again.

176
00:12:37,085 --> 00:12:40,595
The comparison between your two
lists is the result of the lab.

177
00:12:42,435 --> 00:12:44,083
Five mistakes to watch for.

178
00:12:44,423 --> 00:12:49,216
Reading "no badge" as "nothing wrong". Root
cause: without vigilant mode an unsigned

179
00:12:49,256 --> 00:12:53,992
commit shows no status, so a forged unsigned
commit looks like every other unsigned

180
00:12:54,032 --> 00:12:54,394
commit.

181
00:12:54,734 --> 00:12:58,463
Registering a key for authentication
and expecting "Verified".

182
00:12:58,503 --> 00:13:03,387
Root cause: a signing key is a separate
registration, even for the same key file.

183
00:13:03,727 --> 00:13:07,000
Requiring signed commits and
keeping "Rebase and merge".

184
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:11,969
Root cause: a rebase creates new commits,
the old signatures cannot be carried over,

185
00:13:12,009 --> 00:13:15,518
and GitHub has no private key
of yours to sign the new ones.

186
00:13:15,858 --> 00:13:19,331
Revoking a stolen key and
trusting the badges afterwards.

187
00:13:19,371 --> 00:13:24,447
Root cause: verification records persist,
and GitHub does not re-verify old commits

188
00:13:24,487 --> 00:13:26,111
when a key's state changes.

189
00:13:26,451 --> 00:13:29,934
Taking "Verified" on a squash
commit as the author's signature.

190
00:13:29,974 --> 00:13:34,632
Root cause: commits made in the web interface
are signed by GitHub with its own key,

191
00:13:34,672 --> 00:13:36,309
on behalf of a signed-in user.

192
00:13:38,149 --> 00:13:43,083
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
turns on the "require signed commits" rule

193
00:13:43,123 --> 00:13:47,818
for main after an audit. The repository
allows squash merges and rebase merges.

194
00:13:47,858 --> 00:13:52,792
On Monday, squash merges work as before. A
developer who prefers "rebase and merge"

195
00:13:52,832 --> 00:13:57,646
finds the method unusable on main. Another,
working from a fresh laptop without a

196
00:13:57,686 --> 00:14:02,081
signing key, can't get a pull request
merged with the commits as they are.

197
00:14:02,421 --> 00:14:06,958
Nothing is broken. The rule checks the
commits that aren't reachable from other

198
00:14:06,998 --> 00:14:11,651
branches, squash commits pass because
GitHub signs them, and rebase-merge commits

199
00:14:11,691 --> 00:14:15,127
are documented to be created
without signature verification.

200
00:14:15,167 --> 00:14:19,936
The team's decision is then explicit: squash
only on that branch, signing set up on

201
00:14:19,976 --> 00:14:24,745
every machine before the rule goes live,
and one sentence in the contributing guide

202
00:14:24,785 --> 00:14:28,800
that says what the badge on a squash
commit means and what it doesn't.

203
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:37,226
Your turn. Do Lab 24.3, "What GitHub shows
for unsigned, signed and forged commits",

204
00:14:37,266 --> 00:14:42,196
in lab-manual/m24-signing-github.md.
Part A runs in labs/shell.

205
00:14:42,236 --> 00:14:46,613
Part B in your normal shell
on your practice repository.

206
00:14:46,953 --> 00:14:51,763
Predict first, on paper: for each of the
three commits you push, what Git records,

207
00:14:51,803 --> 00:14:56,672
what GitHub displays by default, and what
GitHub displays after you enable vigilant

208
00:14:56,712 --> 00:14:57,795
mode. Then compare.

209
00:14:58,135 --> 00:15:04,692
The challenge is Exercise 24.6,
"Verified, and she was on a plane", in

210
00:15:04,732 --> 00:15:07,330
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

211
00:15:11,170 --> 00:15:16,338
Answer out loud first. A strong answer takes
the three parts in order and labels the

212
00:15:16,378 --> 00:15:21,298
layer each time. For the display: say what
mechanism produces an avatar and what

213
00:15:21,338 --> 00:15:26,443
that mechanism checks. For the evidence:
use the four questions from video 138, and

214
00:15:26,483 --> 00:15:28,551
say where each answer is recorded.

215
00:15:28,891 --> 00:15:33,734
For the controls: separate what flags from
what prevents, and say who has to switch

216
00:15:33,774 --> 00:15:37,557
each one on. The follow-up adds a
"Verified" badge to the commit.

217
00:15:37,597 --> 00:15:42,322
Answer with the one thing a verified
signature proves and the things from section

218
00:15:42,362 --> 00:15:43,969
21B.7 that it still doesn't.

219
00:15:45,809 --> 00:15:49,298
Let's land this. So what do you
tell the security reviewer?

220
00:15:49,338 --> 00:15:53,724
A badge says that a key registered on
an account signed this exact commit.

221
00:15:53,764 --> 00:15:55,877
It doesn't say who typed the change.

222
00:15:56,217 --> 00:15:57,877
You should now be able to say:

223
00:15:58,217 --> 00:16:03,018
The badge is GitHub's judgment about a
signature and a key registered on an account;

224
00:16:03,058 --> 00:16:07,859
an unsigned commit shows no badge unless
the named person has enabled vigilant mode.

225
00:16:08,199 --> 00:16:12,996
A verification record persists after key
rotation and after revocation; a stolen

226
00:16:13,036 --> 00:16:15,777
key's commits are found
by date and audit log.

227
00:16:16,117 --> 00:16:20,691
GitHub signs web-interface commits, including
merge commits and squash commits, with

228
00:16:20,731 --> 00:16:21,351
its own key.

229
00:16:21,691 --> 00:16:26,215
"Rebase and merge" creates commits without
signature verification, so it cannot be

230
00:16:26,255 --> 00:16:28,497
used under a require-signed-commits rule.

231
00:16:28,837 --> 00:16:33,225
"Verified" does not say the change is
correct or reviewed, that the key holder is

232
00:16:33,265 --> 00:16:37,106
who you think, or that the released
artifact came from this repository.

